"""Simulated extractives community intervention practicum."""


def build(course, module):
    return course(104, 'Extractives Community Intervention Practicum',
        'Extractives Community\nIntervention\nPracticum',
        'Produce and defend a phased, reviewable intervention for fictional Afirin Ridge using assessment, referral, engagement and accountability evidence.', [
        module('Assemble a case evidence brief',
            'Combine different evidence streams and identify priority questions, affected people and decision owners.',
            """The final practicum brings several observations together without collapsing them into a single story. Work from the fictional Afirin Ridge file. Early case notes report route delays near a project, a future closure threatens paid and informal work, and a separate service complaint remains unresolved. Treat each issue according to its evidence and urgency. Begin with a table of affected groups, changes reported, records available and questions that could change a decision. [S85]

Add a boundary statement describing place, time and population for each question. Road access may affect residents and mobile vendors at different times. Project employment records may exclude informal suppliers. A caregiver’s report can identify a clinic access barrier while a qualified agency investigates possible environmental harm. Document which claims are reported, independently supported or unresolved. Compare facts that refer to the same period and activity, then connect related issues through plausible mechanisms. [S89]

Draw a simple theory of change for one proposed response. If a revised route and suitable timetable enable people to reach a market or service, verify safe access and operating times before measuring changes in attendance or earnings. Identify assumptions, including affordability and uptake. Evidence from the 2026 Ghana and Australia policy comparison encourages a check of social support and work transition alongside physical closure work. Make the assumptions local to the fictional brief. [R96]

Finish with a priority and ownership map. Give early attention to a time-sensitive support need and to evidence that could uncover severe or hard-to-reverse loss. Clarify the practitioner’s part and the roles responsible for clinical, environmental and land decisions. Preserve review options for people who challenge an entry.""",
            'Afirin Ridge has a route complaint, a threatened loss of informal trading and a health concern supported only by testimony.',
            'Create a brief with four affected groups, two observed or reported patterns, three evidence gaps and the authorities or teams responsible.',
            'Give each question a purpose and owner, separate support and causation, and include people whose work is missing from registers.',
            """
Classify sources. | How should the practicum brief record a resident’s first description of a route delay? | Reported experience, with time and place and an appropriate check. | Verified average delay for all users. | A confirmed cause from site testing. | An observed delivery of a remedy. | An account supports enquiry without resolving other evidence questions.
Specify decision boundaries. | A proposal concerns a service trip at two locations and only on market days. How should the brief bound the sample? | By the affected market trips, locations and relevant timing. | By the entire population without a time period. | By office staff only. | By a publication year without a route definition. | The scope must match the decision and pathway.
Map missing work. | Which group needs its own case entry when the employment list excludes regular vendors? | The vendors who depend on project-related foot traffic. | Only permanent supervisors. | The owners of unrelated properties. | A duplicate group of direct employees. | The work dependency extends beyond a payroll list.
Write an assumption. | Which assumption should the brief test when a new shuttle is expected to restore market attendance? | The service runs at the right times at a price affected users can pay. | The shuttle provider has a name. | Every resident travels for exactly the same purpose. | A printed route always proves attendance. | The service needs practical access for the theory of change to hold.
Assign responsibility. | The brief contains a technical contamination question and an agreed service request. How should work be organised? | Refer technical assessment to the qualified body and progress consented support separately. | Delay agreed support until all technical evidence is final. | Use the same practitioner decision as proof of both. | Share personal records publicly to resolve uncertainty. | Separating tasks allows safe action while technical review proceeds.
"""),
        module('Compare and rehearse intervention options',
            'Compare two responses using practical access, protection, feasibility and authority, then test exception cases.',
            """Choose two feasible responses and describe what would happen under the current arrangement. A route adjustment with temporary support can be compared with a new transport service and a separate transition plan. Use the same criteria for each, including practical access, residual loss, operating costs, safeguarding and timing. State which benefits are provisional. Identify the body that would authorise each relevant change and who can influence the decision. [S85] [S87]

Run a tabletop exercise with fictional scenarios. Include a typical case, a person who needs a protected contact, a supplier who missed the eligibility list and a user who cannot attend at the proposed time. Give each scenario an expected result and a person responsible for recording what occurred. Check whether the referral is received, the alternative route actually works and the affected person can ask for independent review. Use the same case definitions when comparing an amended plan. [S86]

Consider a new claim during the exercise. A provider may produce a statement that says a route is operating while people describe being unable to use it. Treat the claim as new evidence with a scope and date. Examine alternative explanations and verification options, then determine whether a qualified review is needed. Do not discard adverse results just because a sponsor prefers the proposed arrangement. Be clear about who can decide and how the response will be communicated. [S88]

Write a conditional recommendation. Name one change that can proceed within the team’s scope and a condition that would pause a wider decision. Include an accessible fallback and a date for retesting observed failures.""",
            'A tabletop trial works for most participants. A vendor missing from the list is refused help, and a confidential callback is redirected to the wrong desk.',
            'Compare two options, label the two failures, assign corrective owners and specify what would justify pausing expansion.',
            'Correct eligibility review and protected contact. Keep the failures in the evidence record and use the same scenarios in the retest.',
            """
Use common criteria. | What makes two intervention options comparable in the practicum? | Assess both against the same affected groups, time period and criteria. | Use net costs for one and turnout for the other. | Remove the current arrangement from the decision context. | Apply access criteria only to the preferred option. | Common criteria and conditions support a fair comparison.
Test exception cases. | Which fictional test complements one successful mainstream shuttle journey? | A worker who cannot pay the proposed fare and needs the early service. | The same journey repeated with unchanged conditions only. | A pricing discussion with no intended user. | A vote asking whether transport matters. | Exceptions reveal who might fail outside the ordinary path.
Protect case contact. | The drill reveals that a private callback is routed to an unsuitable desk. What is the immediate finding? | A confidentiality process failure needing correction and retest. | Completed access because a dial tone was heard. | Proof every referral system is unsafe. | A publishing success because the case reached someone. | The exercise checks whether safeguards work in practice.
Label new evidence. | A sponsor shares a claim of reliable service without a record period. How should the practicum handle it? | Record the claim and ask for dates, scope and corroboration. | Treat it as verified for all users. | Overwrite all user reports. | Assume the claim is false before review. | Qualification keeps the evidence open without overstating it.
Define a pause. | Which condition warrants pausing expanded use of the fictional pathway? | A protected-contact failure remains unresolved after rehearsal. | A corrected logo is awaiting approval. | The team used different pens at the meeting. | One editor wants another page heading. | An unresolved safeguard failure can change whether expansion is responsible.
"""),
        module('Defend the decision and review plan',
            'Present a phased decision memo integrating all eight EIL domains with accountable review.',
            """A decision memo should explain what can proceed, what requires conditions and who must decide. Start with a dated account of the fictional project stage and affected groups. Show why the recommended option handles the most important needs. Include one viable alternative and explain the evidence that could reverse the choice. Give the social work team and qualified authorities distinct roles. Use language appropriate for both a supervisor and a community briefing. [S85]

Include conditions that address livelihood and displacement concerns. Describe which work and resources people may lose, how an alternative would be accessed and which benefit promises require separate verification. State the role of transition timing and support. Add a protected referral route and a separate grievance process, including a way to review a disputed response. Note how the affected people could challenge both the evidence and the implementation plan. [S86] [S87]

Provide a decision table with indicator, group, timing, source, owner and trigger. Connect each line to the underlying decision. A financial commitment can support a question about capacity, while actual access requires evidence about use. For transparency review, distinguish commitments, payments and delivery and cite the proper public records where relevant. Protect case identities while recording evidence privately for authorised review. [S88]

Prepare to defend one finding that supports the memo and one that limits it. Explain how failure cases would be handled and which results might update the plan. Define follow-up dates and communication routes so a reviewer understands when the next decision will be made and what records would substantiate it.""",
            'The sponsor asks for immediate rollout after 8 of 10 routine scenarios succeed. Both attempted cases with the least access fail.',
            'Write a one-page decision with an alternative, permission boundaries, two review measures and a specific condition for retesting.',
            'Use bounded revision or pause. Keep both denominators, verify the protected fallback and state who owns the next review and when.',
            """
State a decision. | Which memo opening supports clear action after the tabletop reveals unresolved problems? | Describe the bounded revision, decision owner and condition for reconsideration. | List all scenario titles without a recommendation. | Request a launch relying only on majority results. | Mark every decision as already authorised. | A good memo states the action and its conditions.
Preserve denominators. | Eight of ten routine cases succeed and zero of two low-access cases succeed. What evidence should the memo show? | Both group results with their denominators. | Ten successes without individual groups. | A single claim that every scenario worked. | The routine count with the low-access results erased. | Disaggregated outcomes reveal a material access failure.
Assign monitoring. | Which monitoring line can an authorised reviewer act on? | Group, period, evidence source, named owner and adjustment trigger. | An objective with no time or owner. | A cost estimate with no action linked to it. | Only a headline on the number of meetings. | A traceable line connects evidence and a decision.
Connect review to authority. | Who should decide whether a technical restriction can be lifted in the fictional memo? | The authorised qualified decision-maker using their applicable review criteria. | The exercise facilitator based on presentation quality. | The provider based only on commercial need. | The first stakeholder to attend the review. | Technical decisions need the appropriate authority and evidence.
Explain reversible evidence. | What should a learner include when defending a conditional recommendation? | The finding or failed assumption that would change the choice. | A commitment never to change the proposed route. | A claim that no future evidence is relevant. | Only the benefits observed in routine cases. | Reviewable decisions make thresholds and uncertainties explicit.
"""),
    ])
